                    briefing_text_r2:       `Now lead the strategy. The prior plan named three priority segments — yet sales spent <strong>70% of its time on a fourth, low-fit segment</strong> with short contracts, because comp rewarded logo count. Build a focused, customer-centric plan: choose which segments to win and which to <em>deliberately lose</em>, lead with the customer's outcome rather than your feature list, and set a demand target. Remember the trap: asking for more leads while you ignore 78% of the ones you have is pouring water into a leaking bucket — on a frozen budget. State what marketing will <strong>stop</strong> doing to fund the focus.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Align sales execution (Sales Driven)`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `The plan is only a slide until sales drives it. Operationalize alignment: define a shared <strong>“qualified lead”</strong> and a two-way <strong>SLA</strong> (response-time commitment plus a feedback loop), redesign <strong>comp</strong> to reward fit and the priority segments over raw logo count, and set a <strong>value-selling</strong> motion anchored on the plan's value proposition. Then handle the skeptical Sales VP: <em>“your leads are tourists”</em> is the objection. A plan sales nods at but does not own reverts to old habits in six weeks — alignment is <strong>negotiated and incentivized, not announced</strong>.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Reallocate the flat budget & recover`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Reallocate the frozen <strong>C$9.6M</strong> across trade shows (was C$1.4M), brand/content (C$1.1M), digital demand-gen (C$0.8M) and SDR capacity (C$0.5M). The total cannot rise — somebody loses budget. Make the spend <strong>follow your chosen segments</strong>, not channel fashion: your priority segment buys through industry events and references, not paid search. Project the conversion lift and growth, then commit to a <strong>single shared marketing–sales scorecard</strong> — because if both teams report to separate KPIs they can each “win” while the company misses its growth target. Deliver the board pitch.`,

