                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_handoff:       `<strong>That's the leak.</strong> 78% of 2,000 leads never touched is the marketing-to-sales handoff failing, not a volume problem. Now name <em>why</em> the handoff fails — and don't fix it by buying more leads.`,
                    coach_r1_volume:        `<strong>“We need more leads” is the most expensive wrong answer.</strong> You already ignore 78% of the 2,000 you have and convert 9% — generating 3,000 just pours more water into a leaking bucket, and the budget is frozen. Where is the leak, not how big is the top?`,
                    coach_r1_close:        `<strong>The 18% win rate is real, but it isn't the largest leak.</strong> The biggest hole is upstream: 78% of leads never reach a close at all. “Negotiate harder” doesn't touch the handoff.`,
                    coach_r1_product:       `<strong>This isn't a product problem.</strong> Reps close the deals they self-source — the platform wins when it's actually worked. The leak is go-to-market: the handoff between your plan and the pipeline.`,
                    coach_r1_effort:        `<strong>“Sales is lazy” is the diagnosis that guarantees failure.</strong> Reps rationally ignore leads with no shared definition and a comp plan that pays for logo count. Push them harder on the same broken handoff and you'll just deepen the war.`,
                    coach_r1_luck:          `<strong>This isn't a cycle.</strong> Growth halved while CAC climbed and the handoff bled 78% of leads — that's structural misalignment, not a soft market. Diagnose the seam, not the weather.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You found the handoff leak AND named the real root cause: no shared lead definition and a comp plan that pays for the wrong thing. That's the seam between the plan and the pipeline — and it's fixable without a dollar of new budget.`,
                    coach_r1_lowrigor:      `<strong>You're proposing before you've quantified.</strong> If you can't put a number on what the untouched 78% costs, sales will treat your plan as opinion. Quantify the handoff leak first — that number is your mandate.`,
                    coach_r2_three:         `<strong>Three priority segments with the low-fit fourth dropped — that's the discipline.</strong> Customer-centric doesn't mean every customer; it means choosing them. Now make sure sales' comp actually pulls reps toward these three, not the easy logos.`,
                    coach_r2_two:           `<strong>Two best-fit segments is sharp focus.</strong> You're deliberately losing the rest to win the ones that compound — bold, and defensible if your coverage and comp follow. Just confirm two is enough pipeline to hit the number.`,
                    coach_r2_one:           `<strong>One segment is focus taken to a risky extreme.</strong> The discipline is choosing, but a single niche leaves you exposed if it stalls. Customer-centric is a portfolio of chosen customers, not a single bet.`,
                    coach_r2_all4:          `<strong>“We'll serve everyone” means none of them is priority.</strong> Keep all four and reps default to the easiest logos, not the best-fit ones — exactly the behavior that broke the prior plan. Which segment will you <em>lose on purpose</em>?`,
                    coach_r2_product:       `<strong>Product-centric positioning is what made marketing a “campaign factory”.</strong> Leading with your feature list is why reps call the leads tourists. Lead with the chosen segment's outcome — that's what the plan is supposed to change.`,
                    coach_r2_price:         `<strong>Competing on price wins the wrong logos faster.</strong> Discounting attracts short-contract, low-fit buyers — the very fourth segment that ate 70% of sales' time. Customer-centric value, not the cheapest price.`,
                    coach_r2_fewer:         `<strong>Fewer, better-qualified leads is the conversion play.</strong> Aiming 1,400 leads only at priority segments beats 2,000 scattershot when 78% go untouched. Quality of fit, not quantity of volume.`,
                    coach_r2_morevolume:    `<strong>3,000 leads on a frozen budget while you ignore 78% of 2,000?</strong> That's the leaking-bucket error, and it asks for money the board already froze. Fix conversion and the handoff before you turn up the tap.`,
                    coach_r2_nostop:        `<strong>A plan that stops nothing is a wish list.</strong> On a frozen budget, focus has to be funded by cutting off-plan activity. If you stop nothing, the new priorities get no real resource and the old campaign factory rolls on.`,
                    coach_r2_overcut:       `<strong>You've cut almost all off-plan activity.</strong> Funding focus is right, but starving top-of-funnel before the conversion fix lands risks an air-pocket in pipeline. Cut to fund the plan, not to gut demand.`,
                    coach_r3_sla:           `<strong>A two-way SLA is what closes the 78% gap.</strong> A shared “qualified lead”, a response-time commitment, and a feedback loop both sides own turns the handoff from a complaint into a contract. This is the seam the whole plan hinges on.`,
                    coach_r3_oneway:        `<strong>A one-way commitment is half an SLA.</strong> Marketing promising volume with no shared definition and no feedback loop is exactly today's failure with a new label. Alignment is two-way or it isn't alignment.`,
                    coach_r3_nosla:         `<strong>No change to the handoff means no change to the result.</strong> “Trust reps to work the good ones” is what produced 78% untouched. Without a shared definition and an SLA, your beautiful plan dies at the handoff.`,
                    coach_r3_quota:         `<strong>Lifting quota on the same broken comp design just adds pressure to the wrong behavior.</strong> Reps will hit the number the fastest way — easy logos — not the priority segments. Change what comp pays for, not just how much.`,
                    coach_r3_logos:         `<strong>Comp on raw logo count is the engine of the misalignment.</strong> While you pay for volume, reps will chase the easy fourth segment no matter how good your plan reads. Pay for fit, or the plan loses to the comp plan every time.`,
                    coach_r3_discount:      `<strong>Discount-selling protects logo count and destroys the value proposition.</strong> Trading price is the opposite of value-selling on the customer's outcome — it trains the market to wait for the discount and erodes the win you're trying to lift.`,
                    coach_r3_owned:         `<strong>Now sales owns it.</strong> A real SLA, comp that pays for fit, value-selling, AND a Sales VP who helped build it — that's “Sales Driven”. The plan won't revert in six weeks because the reps are paid and managed to run it.`,
                    coach_r3_nobuyin:       `<strong>You presented the plan and they nodded.</strong> Six weeks later reps are back on the fourth segment because that's what comp pays. A plan sales doesn't own is a slide, not a strategy — what did you actually change about how they're paid and managed?`,
                    coach_r4_even:          `<strong>Spreading evenly changes nothing.</strong> A flat budget reallocated flat is the status quo with extra steps. The freeze is the discipline — move the money to where your chosen segments actually buy, or you've proven the board's point.`,
                    coach_r4_fashion:       `<strong>You reallocated by channel fashion, not by segment.</strong> Cutting events for paid search looks modern, but your priority segment buys through industry events and references. Does this budget follow the plan, or follow the trend?`,
                    coach_r4_shared:        `<strong>One shared, revenue-anchored scorecard is the answer to the silo.</strong> Now marketing and sales win or lose on the same number — neither can hit its KPI while the company misses growth. That single metric is what tells the CEO you're aligned.`,
                    coach_r4_siloed:        `<strong>Separate scorecards let both teams “win” while the company loses.</strong> Marketing's “leads up 20%” and sales' “quota flat” can both be true the same quarter growth stalls. What's the <em>shared</em> metric that makes them win together?`,
                    coach_r4_nosdr:         `<strong>You funded the plan but starved the SDR line.</strong> With almost no capacity to work the leads, the SLA you designed in Round 3 can't be honored — the 78% stays untouched. Alignment needs hands to execute it, not just a policy.`,

