                    insight_engine:         `One revenue engine: you diagnosed the handoff, built a focused customer-centric plan, made sales own its execution, and reallocated the frozen budget toward the chosen segments under one shared scorecard. Marketing led, sales drove, and they grew on alignment — not new money.`,
                    insight_volume:         `Leaking bucket: you leaned on generating more leads while 78% went untouched and conversion stayed at 9%. On a frozen budget, more volume into a broken funnel is the most expensive way to stay flat — the leak was always the handoff.`,
                    insight_noownership:    `A slide, not a strategy: the plan may have been elegant, but sales never owned it. Without a real SLA, fit-based comp, and a negotiated Sales VP buy-in, execution reverts to the easy logos in six weeks — “Sales Driven” means incentivized, not announced.`,
                    insight_partial:        `Partial alignment: the plan and the pipeline are closer but not yet one system. Some of the seam — definitions, incentives, or the scorecard — is still open enough for marketing and sales to drift apart again.`,
                    insight_nodeprioritize: `You kept too many segments “priority”, so none truly was — reps default to the easiest logos, not the best-fit ones. Customer-centric requires choosing customers and losing some on purpose.`,
                    insight_siloed:         `You left marketing and sales on separate scorecards, so both can “win” while the company misses growth. The single shared, revenue-anchored metric is what makes them win or lose together.`,
                    insight_revenue_strong: `Strong revenue lift: better conversion and a sales-owned plan grew ARR well above the 8% base — proof that alignment, not budget, was the constraint. The flat budget grew the business by working better, not bigger.`,
                    insight_revenue_flat:   `Revenue stayed flat: the engine never converted alignment into growth. Either the handoff stayed leaky, the plan stayed unfocused, or sales never drove it — the C$9.6M produced the same 8% it always did.`,
                    insight_handoff_closed: `The handoff is closed: high alignment plus healthy conversion means the 78% untouched-lead gap is no longer bleeding the plan. That is the single biggest financial proof that the two departments are now one.`,

                    hint_volume:            `You're chasing volume into a leaking bucket. Stop generating more leads and fix the handoff and conversion — 78% untouched at 9% conversion is the real constraint, not the size of the top of funnel.`,
                    hint_deprioritize:      `You haven't deprioritized a segment yet. Customer-centric means choosing customers — pick which segment to lose on purpose so coverage and comp can concentrate on the ones that compound.`,
                    hint_ownership:         `Sales doesn't own the plan. Without a real SLA, fit-based comp, and a negotiated Sales VP buy-in, execution will revert to the easy logos. Alignment is negotiated and incentivized, not announced.`,
                    hint_siloed:            `Marketing and sales are on separate scorecards — both can “win” while growth stalls. Commit to one shared, revenue-anchored metric so they win or lose together.`,
                    hint_strong:            `Strong position — alignment and conversion are both healthy. Lock it in: make the spend follow the chosen segments and report to one shared scorecard to hold the gain.`,

